            <ArticleTitle>Scoliosis associated with lumbar spondylolisthesis: a case presentation and review of the literature.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>321-4</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND CONTEXT" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">The association between scoliosis and spondylolisthesis is well documented, but criteria and methods for managing the deformity in patients with debilitating pain are not clear.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="PURPOSE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To describe correction of scoliosis by internal fixation of the spondylolisthesis.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="STUDY DESIGN" NlmCategory="METHODS">Case report of a patient with scoliosis developing in association with spondylolisthesis.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">A 17-year-old female presented with a 6-month history of back and leg pain. No spinal curvature was noted in radiographs taken at the onset of symptoms. Significant curvature appeared during the 6-month history of pain in association with an L5-S1 spondylolisthesis. The spondylolisthesis was repaired with posterior pedicle screw instrumentation.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">The pain and the spinal curvature almost completely resolved after surgery, and the outcome remained excellent 14 months after surgery.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">In the properly diagnosed patient, surgical repair of the spondylolisthesis can relieve pain and correct spinal curvature.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
